It appears that the issue (or at least one issue) is a change of  
behavior of gcc in the newest version (4.3.1). Many standard C++  
library include files have been edited down to remove some things, so  
code that used to compile with the old header files are now missing  
some items (which can then be added in). [You'd think a standard  
header would have been standardized before now, but anyway]. More info  
is at http://gcc.gnu.org/gcc-4.3/porting_to.html . I think the NCL may  
need to be updated to work (it will compile under gcc 4.0 but not  
4.3), then phylobase should work.

Incidentally, the person who found this was Jon Auman, NESCent's  
sysadmin, when he was investigating what info I'd need from R-Forge to  
replicate their build environment. It's even more impressive when you  
find out he's not really an R or C programmer.

Brian


